217-## Part 12 — Brand-level data: hard firewall
218-
219-Brand-specific lab results, COAs, and internal test data do not live in this wiki. This wiki is the public-facing knowledge base behind heavymetalindex.com, the HMT&C program's published criteria, and courses. Brand-identifying contamination data in any of those channels creates legal and commercial risk.
220-
221-There is no `wiki/brands/` directory. If a source names brands in its findings (Consumer Reports rankings, HBBF brand tables, FDA recall notices), summarize the category-level and ingredient-level signal on the relevant `products/` or `ingredients/` page. Do not create brand pages and do not list brand-by-brand values in the wiki.
222-
223-Recalls and enforcement actions are fine to name on `regulations/` and `products/` pages because they are already public record, but frame them as regulatory events, not brand rankings.
224-
225-Published third-party testing (Consumer Reports, HBBF, etc.) can be cited as sources, and their methodology can be discussed, but do not reproduce their brand-level tables into the wiki. Link to the source page instead.
226-
227-COAs and internal lab data belong in the private brand-intelligence build described in Part 26, not here. If Karen drops a COA into `raw/` by accident, flag it and ask before ingesting.
228-
229-HMT&C certified brands are never mentioned in the wiki. Not in page bodies, not in examples, not in case studies. The wiki is evidentially separate from the certification program; treating it as promotional infrastructure for HMT&C would destroy the defensibility argument this whole project rests on.
230-
231-Brand-firewall calls are usually unambiguous from the source's framing: if the source names a brand and the wiki page is a product-category or ingredient page, summarize the category-level signal and drop the brand name. The ask-Karen case is narrow: a source whose findings *only* make sense with brand attribution (e.g., a single-brand recall whose entire scope is one product line, where a category-level summary would misrepresent the source). In that case, ask Karen before writing. In all other cases, decide and proceed.
