297:## Part 19 — HMT&C threshold-setting workflow (staff-only outputs)
298-
299-This workflow produces certification thresholds. **Its outputs are staff and HMT&C certification content, not public wiki content, and they do not live inside `wiki/`.** Per the wiki/HMT&C firewall in Part 2, percentile-selection arithmetic (any P-value computation), clean/dirty subcategory designations, pooling math, sensitivity bands, and final candidate values appear under `data/workbench/standards/` and on the local staff Standards Workbench tool — not on heavymetalindex.com and not in `wiki/`. Public product-category pages render only the literature-native view per [[docs/page-templates]] product-category template.
300-
301-### The locked methodology
302-
303-The methodology is fixed, system-wide, and applies to every category and every row in the index. It is not infant-and-child-foods-specific.
304-
305-**Per-analyte standard for a clean subcategory = P97** of that subcategory's marginal occurrence distribution for that analyte.
306-
307-**Per-analyte standard for a dirty subcategory = P45** of that subcategory's marginal occurrence distribution for that analyte.
308-
309-**Per-analyte standard for an independent (no within-pair partner) row = P97** of that row's marginal distribution, treated as clean-by-default. Override slot exists for rows where evidence justifies dirty treatment.
310-
311-**Each row's standard is in the native basis of the product form.** Powder rows use powder-as-placed-on-market. RTF liquid rows use as-consumed. Dry cereal rows use dry-as-sold. Puree rows use as-consumed. Sources reporting in other bases are converted into the row's native basis during pooling, never the other way around. The 1:7 reconstitution factor lives in `tools/evidence/basis-conversion.mjs`.
312-
313-**Final HMT&C value = `min(per-analyte percentile, lowest applicable regulatory ceiling)`** with both in the row's native basis. Auto-emitted rationale tag is `literature-baseline` when the pooled percentile binds and `regulatory-alignment` when the cap binds.
314-
315-**ALARA + the §80th-percentile ratchet trigger** (per Governing Principles §"Two Percentile Mechanisms") drive tightening *after* publication. Remediation plans handle initial-publication brands above the limit. The standards-setting math here is for initial publication only.
316-
317-### Rationale tagging on staged standards
318-
319-When a decision is staged in the workbench, one rationale tag is required:
320-
321-- `literature-baseline` — the pooled per-analyte percentile is the binding value.
322-- `regulatory-alignment` — the lowest applicable regulatory cap is the binding value.
323-- `precautionary` — staff selects a value lower than both the pooled percentile and the regulatory cap, with documented rationale.
324-- `feasibility-driven` — staff selects a value higher than the pooled percentile because current methods or supply-chain realities do not support the tighter value, paired with a sunset plan.
325-
326-The gap between literature-baseline and the final value is stated honestly. Failing to name the rationale is the lint failure.
327-
328-See [[docs/standards-methodology]] for the pool admission rules, pooling math, sensitivity-band thresholds, the row-pass calibration math behind P97/P45, and the clean/dirty subcategory classification framework. See [[docs/schema-history]] for the deprecated "clean-platform / contaminated-platform" P90/P10/P20 framework.
329-
